  "conclusion": {
    "hypothesis_supported": true,
    "summary": "340M shows non-monotonic cross-architecture convergence on LAMBADA (rho=-0.5, perm_p=0.45), contrasting with 1.3B monotonic convergence (rho=-1.0, perm_p=0.0167). Rebound confirmed at h3-1 (std +0.005 from h6-1) and also at h12-1 (std +0.006 from h24-1). The rebound is driven by differential architecture responses: GDN degrades with attention (net=-256 at h3-1) while HGRN2 improves (net=+238), creating divergence instead of convergence.",
    "key_findings": [
      "340M cross-arch std is non-monotonic: pure(0.0248) > h24-1(0.0116) < h12-1(0.0178) > h6-1(0.0110) < h3-1(0.0160)",
      "Two rebound points: h12-1 and h3-1, where adding more attention increases rather than decreases cross-arch divergence",
      "340M Spearman rho=-0.5 (perm_p=0.45) vs 1.3B rho=-1.0 (perm_p=0.0167): scale is necessary for monotonic convergence",
      "Rebound mechanism: differential response to attention \u2014 GDN hurt by attention at high ratios, HGRN2 helped",
      "340M h3-1 per-example Jaccard (helped=0.094, hurt=0.090) comparable to 1.3B exp-010 values (~0.096, ~0.090), suggesting similar sample-level mechanisms but different aggregate outcomes",
      "Scale as necessary condition confirmed: 340M capacity insufficient for attention to equalize architectures at high ratios"
    ],
    "implications": "The capacity-limited rebound supports the paper thesis that attention-recurrent interaction requires sufficient model capacity (1.3B+) to achieve full cross-architecture equalization. At 340M, the models lack capacity to accommodate high attention ratios, leading to architecture-specific degradation patterns."
